 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The first Europeans to reach the Cameroon coast were the Portuguese. About 1472 the sailor Fernando Pó (Fernão do Pó) entered the Wouri estuary. His men saw many prawns (shrimps) in the river and named it Rio dos Camarões, the “river of prawns”. From this name came “Cameroon”.\nThe island of Bioko was long called Fernando Po after him.\nTrade: the Portuguese, then the Dutch (17th century), the English and the French, traded with coastal peoples, especially the Duala, who acted as middlemen between the Europeans and the peoples inland.\n• Europeans brought cloth, alcohol, guns, gunpowder, mirrors, beads and salt.\n• They bought ivory, slaves, and later palm oil and palm kernels.\nEffects: the slave trade took many people; new goods and crops (maize, cassava); coastal chiefs grew rich and powerful; Christian missions came later (the Baptists in 1841–1845).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "n": "Rio dos Camarões"
  },
  "problem": "Explain where the name Cameroon comes from.",
  "steps": [
   "Portuguese sailors under Fernando Pó saw many prawns in the Wouri estuary.",
   "They called it {n}, the river of prawns; the name became Cameroon."
  ],
  "answer": "From Rio dos Camarões, the Portuguese name for the river of prawns.",
  "checks": []
 },
